---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — CloudOps Guard"
  description="A truthful description of what this website's current implementation does and does not do with data, as of v0.3.0 -- before any production deployment."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <p class="status-label status-label--neutral">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        This page describes what this website's implementation actually does today. It is not a legal
        privacy policy, and it makes no promise beyond what is described below.
      </p>

      <h2>Interactive demonstrations</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/demo/kubernetes">Kubernetes</a> and <a href="/demo/gitlab">GitLab</a> demonstration
        pages use fixed, synthetic example data for a fictional project. This data is not real; it does
        not describe any actual cluster, GitLab project, person, or organization.
      </p>

      <h2>The local report explorer</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>report.json</code> file you select in the <a href="/explorer">local report explorer</a> is
        read and rendered entirely in your browser tab, and is <strong>never uploaded anywhere</strong>. See <a href="/learn/local-report-privacy"
          >how the local report explorer keeps your report private</a
        > for the exact mechanics.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact and feedback</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/request-demo">Requesting a pilot</a> and <a href="/feedback">sending feedback</a> work
        differently: text you deliberately type into one of those two forms and submit &mdash; your name,
        work email, optional company, and message &mdash; is intentionally <strong
          >transmitted and retained as an email</strong
        > so CloudOps Guard can respond to you. This is the opposite of the local report explorer's
        browser-only boundary above, and the two are never connected: no code path on this site can send
        an imported report, a finding, or any report-derived content to the contact/feedback path, and
        neither form offers a way to attach one. The only anti-abuse mechanism on these two forms is
        Cloudflare Turnstile's verification token; no report, file, credential, kubeconfig, GitLab token,
        or other infrastructure secret field exists on either form, and both forms warn against entering
        one in the message field regardless. No acknowledgement email is ever sent back to the address you
        provide.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No analytics, session replay, or third-party tracking script runs on any page.</li>
        <li>No endpoint on this site accepts an uploaded report, file, or credential.</li>
        <li>
          Neither contact form accepts a report, an attachment, or any credential/token other than
          Turnstile's own verification token.
        </li>
        <li>No acknowledgement email is sent to the address you submit in a contact/feedback form.</li>
        <li>No production deployment of this v0.3.0 website has occurred.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Content-Security-Policy</h2>
      <p>
        The routes that can hold report-derived data &mdash; <a href="/demo/kubernetes">/demo/kubernetes</a>, <a href="/demo/gitlab">/demo/gitlab</a>, and <a href="/explorer">/explorer</a> &mdash; are served with a restrictive Content-Security-Policy, including <code>connect-src 'none'</code>, as a browser-enforced backstop against any network request those pages' code might otherwise issue. <a href="/request-demo">/request-demo</a> and <a href="/feedback">/feedback</a> are served with a separate, dedicated Content-Security-Policy that permits only this site itself and Cloudflare's official Turnstile origin (<code>challenges.cloudflare.com</code>) &mdash; the one external script permitted anywhere on this site.
      </p>

      <h2>A separate, future report-ingestion API</h2>
      <p>
        A versioned report-ingestion API and a command-line uploader are specified in a design document
        (v0.4.0). Later phases have added local, in-memory reference storage code, real Argon2id
        authentication mechanics, a working implementation of the four designed API endpoints, and, most
        recently, the customer-controlled command-line uploader itself &mdash; but strictly as
        <strong>local/staging-only</strong> code: both the API and the uploader exist only as code a
        developer can run themselves against a loopback address for testing, and uploading is never
        automatic even then (it requires an explicit, typed confirmation or an equivalent flag). There is
        still no network-reachable or customer-reachable server, no real customer credential, and nothing
        deployed anywhere. That design is, and will remain, a service entirely separate from this website:
        nothing on this site can or will call it, opening a report in the local report explorer above will
        never trigger it, and this website's own behavior described on this page does not change because
        of it. See the <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a> for its current status.
      </p>

      <p><a href="/roadmap">See the roadmap</a> for what is planned or still absent.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
